						    <h1 class="page-title ">
        Measure V    </h1>

							
							
<div class="entry-content">
	<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>MEASURE V</strong><br />
<strong>WEST CONTRA COSTA UNIFIED SCHOOL DISTRICT</strong></p>
<p>Shall the measure supporting local quality education in West Contra Costa Unified School District, protecting core academics, science, music, arts, and career technical programs that prepare students for the changing economy; attracting and retaining qualified teachers; and maintaining mental health services and counselors to support student success; by renewing the parcel tax at the existing rate of 7.2¢/building square foot, raising approximately $10,238,737 annually for nine years, with senior exemptions, independent citizen oversight, audits, and local control, be adopted?</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>COUNTY COUNSEL’S IMPARTIAL ANALYSIS OF</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE V</strong></p>
<p>The governing board of the West Contra Costa Unified School District has adopted a resolution proposing to extend its current parcel tax (a qualified special tax). This ballot measure asks voters to decide whether an existing parcel tax should continue to be imposed on parcels of taxable real property within the District for nine years, beginning July 1, 2027 and expiring on June 30, 2036.</p>
<p>If the measure passes, the tax would apply to each “improved parcel” of taxable real property in the District. An improved parcel, property with a building on it, would be taxed at a rate of 7.2 cents per square foot of total building area on the parcel. For example, the annual tax on a parcel with a 2,000 square-foot building would be $144.</p>
<p>Based on information from the County Auditor-Controller’s Office, the current parcel tax applying the same rate of 7.2 cents per square foot of total building area per parcel is set to expire on June 30, 2027.</p>
<p>The ballot measure includes certain exemptions. Upon proper application to the District, the District may exempt any parcel from the parcel tax that is owned and occupied as a principal residence by (1) a person 65 years of age or older; (2) a person receiving Supplemental Security Income for a disability; or (3) a person receiving Social Security Disability Insurance benefits, whose yearly income does not exceed 250 percent of the 2012 federal poverty guidelines issued by the United States Department of Health and Human Services.</p>
<p>The ballot measure states that the proceeds of the parcel tax will be used for “protecting core academics, science, music, arts and career technical programs that prepare students for the changing economy; attracting and retaining qualified teachers; and maintaining mental health services and counselors to support student success.” Proceeds from the parcel tax may be used only for the specific purposes set forth in the ballot measure and according to constitutional and statutory provisions.</p>
<p>State law requires the District’s chief fiscal officer to file an annual report with the District’s Board of Education that states the amounts of funds collected and expended in each year and the status of any projects required or authorized to be funded from the parcel tax proceeds. The proceeds from the parcel tax will be deposited into a District account that will be kept separate from other District accounts. Parcel tax funds will be subject to an annual financial audit. The measure also requires the District to have an independent oversight committee to oversee the use of parcel tax proceeds.</p>
<p>Of those voting on the measure, two-thirds must approve the measure for it to pass.</p>
<p>A “yes” vote is in favor of authorizing the parcel tax for a nine-year period.</p>
<p>A “no” vote is against authorizing the parcel tax for a nine-year period.</p>
<hr />
<p>ARGUMENT IN FAVOR OF<br />
MEASURE V</p>
<p>A Yes vote on Measure V will preserve vital funds for local schools. It is not a new tax; it will renew local education funding and continue our investment in West Contra Costa students.</p>
<p>Measure V will continue local funding that has supported our students and our schools since 2004 with no rate increase. Measure V will raise $10.2 million annually. Every dollar remains in our community and cannot be taken by the state or federal government.</p>
<p>California funding has not kept pace with rising costs for special education, employee salaries, utilities, insurance, and essential school operations. Without renewal, our schools face cuts to programs students rely on every day.</p>
<p>A Yes vote helps by:<br />
• Protecting core academics in reading, writing, math, and science<br />
• Maintaining science, technology, engineering, and math (STEM) instruction<br />
• Attracting and retaining qualified teachers<br />
• Providing career and technical training to prepare students for careers in a changing economy<br />
• Maintaining mental health and counseling services<br />
• Supporting visual and performing arts<br />
• Improving campus safety<br />
• Supporting athletic and sports programs<br />
• Supporting after school programs</p>
<p>Measure V includes strong taxpayer protections. Independent citizen oversight ensures funds are spent as promised, with annual audits by a state-approved certified public accountant. Revenue stays local and cannot be used for administrator salaries. Seniors and qualified individuals with disabilities remain eligible for an exemption.</p>
<p>Strong schools build strong communities. They strengthen our neighborhoods, prepare our future workforce, and help make West Contra Costa a community where families and businesses want to invest. We all benefit from strong schools.<br />
Protect what we have built and invest in our future.<br />
Vote Yes on Measure V – vital funds for local schools.</p>
<p>Visit www.VoteYesOnV.com</p>
<p>Francisco Ortiz, Ed.D., President, United Teachers of Richmond<br />
Jen Komaromi, Small Business Owner<br />
Bisa French, Executive Director<br />
Jeffrey Wright, Realtor<br />
Judy Sanders, Retired WCCUSD Educator</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>REBUTTAL TO ARGUMENT IN FAVOR OF</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE V</strong></p>
<p><strong>West Contra Costa Unified School District funding is exceedingly generous</strong> — <span style="text-decoration: underline;">without</span> Measure V’s annual $10.2 million renewal.</p>
<p>● Average class size: 22 students. Average per-student funding: $21,304.<strong> 22 x $21,304 = $468,688.</strong></p>
<p>● $468,688 minus $145,000 (estimated average teacher salary/benefits) =<strong> $323,688 remaining per classroom</strong> — more than twice the teacher’s compensation.</p>
<p>● These are <span style="text-decoration: underline;">operational</span> expenditures, not construction or major renovation costs. Why aren’t these amounts enough for WCCUSD’s delegated tasks (the teaching/learning of beneficial knowledge and skills)?</p>
<p>● After finally paying off 1991’s bankruptcy loan in 2012, WCCUSD overspending has continued. Just last year, WCCUSD had to initiate another “fiscal stabilization plan” to avoid State takeover.</p>
<p><strong>Customary Claims….</strong></p>
<p>● The usual promises, repeated now — independent oversight, audits, dollars staying local — also accompanied previous tax measures, but didn’t prevent recent crises.</p>
<p>● “Oversight” that merely reviews spending after the fact can’t substitute for responsible budgeting management <span style="text-decoration: underline;">before</span> spending occurs.</p>
<p>● WCCUSD’s “Yes vote helps…” items that are merely standard expectations for school systems — particularly already well-funded districts like WCCUSD.</p>
<p>● Good intentions don’t alter WCCUSD’s demonstrated pattern: recurrent financial instability, <span style="text-decoration: underline;">despite lavish funding.</span></p>
<p>● WCCUSD’s “senior exemption” from parcel taxes requires passworded uploading of private documents.</p>
<p>● Meanwhile, fair-minded seniors recognize the inequity in backing taxes they needn’t pay themselves.</p>
<p><strong>Before renewing WCCUSD’s parcel tax, affordability-stressed parents and other taxpayers should require both reliable fiscal management and greatly improved academic performance.</strong></p>
<p>● 2024-25 CAASPP and CAST test scores show only 33.4% of WCCUSD students tested as having “Met or Exceeded Standards” in English, 25.9% in Math, 21.3% in Science.</p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>Measure V? Vote No!</strong></p>
<p>Contra Costa Tax Payers Association, Denise Gianni, Member of Executive Committee<br />
Lyle Miller, Retired Business Owner, San Pablo<br />
Robin MacDonald, Business Owner, Pinole<br />
Marilynne Mellander, Coordinator Save El Sobrante</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>ARGUMENT AGAINST</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE V</strong></p>
<p><strong>Vote No on Measure V.</strong></p>
<p><strong>The West Contra Costa Unified School District (WCCUSD) does not have a revenue problem.</strong></p>
<p>● The 2026-27 budget shows anticipated revenue of $532 Million, with expected spending of $520 Million — an operating surplus of $12 million.</p>
<p>● Since 2016-17, enrollment has declined from 28,518 to 24,423 — more than 4,000 students!</p>
<p>● So WCCUSD will spend $21,304 for each of its 2026-27 estimated 24,423 enrolled students, on record state funding levels. Per-student funding based on projected “Average Daily Attendance” (ADA) of 22,677 students is even higher.</p>
<p>● Measure V would now extend a parcel tax first imposed in 2004 to 2036, having annually taken $10 Million extra for 32 years by then, and guaranteeing the district an <span style="text-decoration: underline;">extra $10 million every year, no matter how much lower WCCUSD enrollments drop.</span></p>
<p><strong>Consider what WCCUSD residents already pay:</strong></p>
<p>● The highest school-bond tax in Contra Costa County, about $254 per $100,000 of assessed value (stacked from bonds passed in 2000, 2002, 2005, 2010, 2012, and 2020), and 3½ times Mt. Diablo Unified’s $72.</p>
<p>● Some of the highest sales taxes in the Bay Area, 10.75% percent in El Cerrito and 9.75% percent in Richmond, against California’s 7.25% statewide base.</p>
<p>● A West Contra Costa Healthcare District parcel tax of $52 per home (higher for other properties), still collected years after Doctors Medical Center closed in 2015.</p>
<p><strong>Now consider the district’s direction:</strong></p>
<p>● Spending has climbed to $520 million in 2026-27 from $377 million in COVID Year 2019-20 (a 38% increase), even as enrollment has fallen 13.5 percent over those years, from 28,247 students then to 24,423 now.</p>
<p>A “No” vote on Measure V takes not a dollar from other record funding the schools already receive. Voting “No” requires a district already running a surplus to live within its means.</p>
<p><strong>Enough is enough. Vote No on V.</strong></p>
<p>Contra Costa Tax Payers Association, Denise Gianni, Member of Executive Committee<br />
Lyle Miller, Retired Business Owner, San Pablo<br />
Robin MacDonald, Business Owner, Pinole<br />
Marilynne Mellander, Coordinator Save El Sobrante</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>REBUTTAL TO ARGUMENT AGAINST</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE V</strong></p>
<p>The argument against Measure V presents a misleading one-year snapshot while ignoring what comes next. WCCUSD’s multi-year projection shows a surplus in 2026-27 but deficits in 2027-28 ($13.9M) and 2028-29 ($30.7M). The 26-27 surplus is from restricted one-time funds that cannot support core educational programs.</p>
<p>The argument against Measure V gets enrollment figures wrong and misrepresents how education funding works. Measure V funds district schools <span style="text-decoration: underline;">and</span> charter schools – a total enrollment of 28,616.</p>
<p>Local declines in enrollment mirror nationwide trends. Declining enrollment does not reduce costs of operating schools, serving students with disabilities, maintaining technology, or retaining educators. <span style="text-decoration: underline;">All of those costs are on the rise.</span></p>
<p>State-level funding for California schools is based on attendance, not enrollment. WCCUSD has significantly improved attendance since 2022, reducing chronic absenteeism by 36%. This bounce-back since the pandemic was made possible by consistent local support of strong public schools.</p>
<p>The opposition lists school bonds, sales tax and a healthcare parcel tax. None of those fund educational services. School bonds only fund facilities.</p>
<p>Analyzing school budgets by treating all expenses the same and dividing by enrollment misleadingly treats restricted grants, employee benefits, and legally required services as flexible classroom spending.</p>
<p>Measure V is not a new tax. By maintaining our only source of local funding for educational services, Measure V protects students’ access to core academics, qualified educators, and safe schools. It maintains senior and disability exemptions, independent oversight, and annual audits.</p>
<p>Protect vital funding for local schools. Vote Yes on V.</p>
<p>Lucas Menanix, DLCAP Chair<br />
Michael Peritz, Retired Educator<br />
Maureen Toms, Pinole City Council Member<br />
Bisa French, Executive Director FJC &amp; Retired Chief of Police<br />
Jeffrey Wright, Realtor</p>
