<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>FULL TEXT OF</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE V</strong></p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;"><strong>FULL TEXT OF BALLOT MEASURE</strong></span></p>
<p><strong>INTRODUCTION</strong></p>
<p>The West Contra Costa Unified School District (“District”) serves over 24,000 students across 56 school sites, ranging from transitional kindergarten through Grade 12. The District is dedicated to providing educational programs designed to help every student achieve their potential.</p>
<p>The District’s community has consistently supported District schools and programs by approving and re-approving, in 2004, 2012, and again in 2016, a local education parcel tax providing critical school funding. The most recent and current measure supporting this parcel tax, Measure T, is set to expire at the end of fiscal year 2026-27. The pending loss of this local funding will only compound existing sources of financial strain on the District, including Statewide and nationwide trends such as declining enrollment, high levels of chronic absenteeism, and increased operational costs due to rising inflation.</p>
<p>If the existing local parcel tax in support of West Contra Costa Unified schools is not continued, the District may be required to implement further cuts to teachers, programs, and student services, in order to maintain fiscal solvency.</p>
<p>Renewing the parcel tax at the existing rate as proposed in this Measure would generate approximately $10,238,737 annually for nine years, providing funds to protect core academic programs including reading, writing, math and science; attract and retain qualified teachers; maintain science, technology, engineering and math (STEM) instruction, including by supporting lab materials and activities; provide career and technical training to make sure students are ready for careers in the changing economy; and maintain mental health services and counseling services to support student success.</p>
<p>Like the prior parcel taxes approved by the District’s voters, this parcel tax renewal is temporary, automatically expiring in nine years, and cannot be renewed without further voter approval.</p>
<p>Renewal of the parcel taxes will continue to ensure that large businesses and large commercial property owners will pay their fair share so the burden does not fall solely on the average homeowner.</p>
<p>Funds from the renewed parcel tax may only be used for the specific purposes described in this Measure. Every cent from the parcel tax must stay local for West Contra Costa schools and cannot be taken away by the State or federal government.</p>
<p>The proposed Measure provides exemptions from the parcel tax for homeowners who are over 65 years old, are receiving Supplemental Security Income for a disability, regardless of age, or receiving Social Security Disability Insurance benefits and qualifying as low income, regardless of age, all to help ensure the cost is not a burden to those on fixed or limited incomes.</p>
<p>The Measure requires strict fiscal accountability protections to ensure funds are spent as promised, including an independent oversight committee, annual audits and public disclosure of all spending.</p>
<p><strong>SUMMARY STATEMENT OF THE MEASURE</strong></p>
<p>Shall the measure supporting local quality education in West Contra Costa Unified School District, protecting core academics, science, music, arts, and career technical programs that prepare students for the changing economy; attracting and retaining qualified teachers; and maintaining mental health services and counselors to support student success; by renewing the parcel tax at the existing rate of 7.2¢/building square foot, raising approximately $10,238,737 annually for nine years, with senior exemptions, independent citizen oversight, audits, and local control, be adopted?</p>
<p><strong>PURPOSE OF THE MEASURE</strong></p>
<p>The District proposes to levy and collect the parcel tax annually for nine (9) years as more fully described below under the heading “RATE AND PROCEDURE,” and to implement accountability measures in connection with the parcel tax to ensure that funds are used only for the following specific purposes:</p>
<p>• Protect core academic programs – reading, writing, math and science</p>
<p>• Attract and retain qualified teachers, including quality science teachers</p>
<p>• Support science laboratories, materials and activities</p>
<p>• Support career technical education and job training for students to prepare them for the workforce</p>
<p>• Support visual and performing arts</p>
<p>• Provide classroom computers and technology</p>
<p>• Improve safety on and around our campuses</p>
<p>• Support athletic and sports programs</p>
<p>• Support after school programs to keep kids away from gangs and drugs</p>
<p>• Maintain mental health services and counseling services to support student success.</p>
<p>In addition to the educational and operational purposes stated above, proceeds from the parcel tax may be used to pay for the cost of<br />
administering the parcel tax, including the cost of preparation of tax rolls annually, providing for annual audits, processing exemption<br />
applications, and all other costs associated with administration and collection of the parcel tax.</p>
<p><strong>RATE AND PROCEDURE</strong></p>
<p>Subject to two-thirds approval of the voters, this local funding measure and its parcel tax will be in effect for nine years commencing July 1, 2027.</p>
<p>Amount of Parcel Tax. The parcel tax will be levied on each parcel of taxable real property (except exempt parcels, as described below), at the following rate:</p>
<p>• <span style="text-decoration: underline;">Improved Parcels</span>: 7.2¢ per square foot of building area on each parcel.</p>
<p>Method of Collection. The parcel tax will be collected by the Contra Costa County Tax Collector (the “County Tax Collector”) at the same time as and along with, and will be subject to the same penalties as, general ad valorem taxes collected by said County Tax Collector. The tax and penalty will bear interest at the same rate as the rate for unpaid ad valorem property taxes until paid. Any tax levied will become a lien upon the properties against which taxes are assessed and collectible as herein provided.</p>
<p>The Board may adopt such additional or supplemental procedures as it deems necessary or convenient for the administration of the parcel tax.</p>
<p>Definition of Parcel; Calculation of Building Square Feet. A “parcel of taxable real property” is defined as any unit of real property within the District’s boundaries that receives a separate tax bill for ad valorem property taxes from the County Tax Collector. Units of real property may be treated as separate parcels of taxable real property if a separate parcel number, assessment number, or similar designation is assigned them by the County Assessor. All property that is otherwise exempt from or on which are levied no ad valorem property taxes in any year, shall also be exempt from the parcel tax in such year. Parcels which are (i) contiguous, (ii) used solely for owner-occupied, single family residential purposes, and (iii) held under identical ownership, may, by annually submitting an application to the District by July 1 of any year, be treated as a single “parcel” for the purpose of the parcel tax.</p>
<p>An “improved parcel” is a parcel upon which one or more permanent buildings, including mobile homes, is/are constructed or situated. An unimproved parcel is a parcel upon which no permanent building, including mobile homes is/are constructed or situated. “Building” means any structure having a roof and enclosing walls that creates enclosed space capable of being entered by persons.</p>
<p>Classification of parcels as improved or unimproved shall be determined as of the January 1 lien date preceding the fiscal year for which the tax is levied, as determined by County records.</p>
<p>For purposes of calculating the parcel tax on an improved parcel, the building square footage shall be the total gross building area of all buildings situated on the parcel or, as in the case of condominium parcels, comprising the parcel. The total square footage of building area constructed on each improved parcel shall be based on data from the County Assessor, City of Richmond, City of El Cerrito, City of San Pablo, City of Pinole, City of Hercules, other municipal assessor or planning/building authority, and/or voluntary disclosure by property owners. The taxable building area square feet per parcel shall be updated annually, with changes as a result of additional building area or demolished building area applying to the calculation for the subsequent tax year only. Changes to building area square feet occurring during a given tax year shall not be prorated for the purposes of the parcel tax calculation for that tax year. Taxpayers wishing to dispute or correct data regarding actual building square feet, must submit corrective data to the District by March 31 in order for corrections to be considered and, if accepted by the District in the District’s reasonable discretion, applied to the following tax year’s calculations.</p>
<p>Exemptions. All property that is otherwise exempt from property taxes levied in any year will also be exempt from the parcel tax in such year. In addition, exemptions shall be available on residential parcels owned and occupied as a principal residence by the following persons, upon application to the District:</p>
<p>• persons who are 65 years or older;</p>
<p>• persons receiving Supplemental Security Income for a disability regardless of age; or</p>
<p>• persons receiving Social Security Disability Insurance benefits, regardless of age, whose yearly income does not exceed 250 percent of the 2012 federal poverty guidelines issued by the United States Department of Health and Human Services.</p>
<p>Persons qualifying for such exemptions must apply to the District for such exemption before July 1 of the applicable tax year, under procedures established by the District or otherwise as required by law or by the Tax Collector. When an exemption from the parcel tax has been granted to a parcel, an application for exemption will not have to be re-submitted for such exempt parcel annually, if the facts are such that said parcel continues to qualify for the exemption described in this Measure.</p>
<p>The District will annually provide to the County Tax Collector or other appropriate County tax officials a list of parcels that the District has approved for an exemption as provided for herein.</p>
<p>With respect to all general property tax matters within its jurisdiction, the County Tax Collector or other appropriate County tax official shall make all final determinations of tax exemption or relief for any reason, and that decision shall be final and binding. With respect to matters specific to the levy of the special tax, including the exemptions therefrom as described herein, the application of the definition of “parcel of taxable real property” to any parcel(s) or any other disputed matter specific to the application of the special tax, the decisions of the District shall be final and binding. The procedures described herein, and any additional procedures established by the Board shall be the exclusive claims procedure for claimants seeking an exemption, refund, reduction, or re-computation of the special tax. Whether any particular claim is to be resolved by the District or by the County shall be determined by the District, in coordination with the County as necessary.</p>
<p><strong>APPROPRIATIONS LIMIT</strong></p>
<p>To the extent required by the California Constitution and applicable laws, the appropriations limit for the District will be adjusted periodically by the aggregate sum collected by levy of this special tax.</p>
<p><strong>ACCOUNTABILITY AND COMPLIANCE MEASURES</strong></p>
<p>In accordance with California Government Code sections 50075.1 and 50075.3, and as proscribed by the Board of Education of the District, the following accountability measures shall apply to the parcel tax levied in accordance with this Measure:</p>
<p>(a) The proceeds of the education parcel tax shall be applied only to the specific purposes identified under the heading “PURPOSE OF THE MEASURE,” above.</p>
<p>(b) The proceeds of the special tax shall be deposited into a fund, which shall be kept separate and apart from other funds of the District.</p>
<p>(c) No later than January 1 of each year while the parcel tax is in effect, the chief fiscal officer of the District shall prepare and file with the Board of Education of the District a report detailing the amount of funds collected and expended, and the status of any project, programs, or purposes identified under the heading “PURPOSE OF THE MEASURE,” above.</p>
<p>(d) The Board will conduct an annual financial audit of the proceeds of the special tax ensuring funds are spent for educational purposes.</p>
<p>(e) The Board shall arrange for independent oversight and review of the District’s expenditures of proceeds of the education parcel tax to ensure they are expended for the purposes set forth under the heading “PURPOSE OF THE MEASURE,” above. Such oversight may be conducted by a committee established by the Board for such purposes, or an existing committee the purposes of which may be expanded by the Board to include such purposes.</p>
<p><strong>PROTECTION OF FUNDING</strong></p>
<p>Current law forbids any decrease in State or federal funding to the District resulting from the adoption of a parcel tax. However, if any such funding is reduced or affected because of the adoption of this local funding measure, then the Board may reduce the amount of the special taxes levied as necessary in order to restore such State or federal funding and/or maximize the District’s fiscal position for the benefit of the District’s educational programs. As a result, whether directly or indirectly, no funding from this Measure may be taken away by the State.</p>
<p><strong>SEVERABILITY</strong></p>
<p>The Board of Education of the District hereby declares, and the voters by approving this Measure concur, that every section, paragraph, sentence and clause of this Measure has independent value, and the Board of Education and the voters would have adopted each provision hereof regardless of every other provision hereof. Upon approval of this Measure by the voters, should any part be found by a court of competent jurisdiction to be invalid for any reason, all remaining parts hereof shall remain in full force and effect to the fullest extent allowed by law.</p>
